        "date":  "2026-07-24",
        "theme":  "Orchestration",
        "summary":  "Karthik Karunanithi argues that Microsoft\u0027s consolidation of Semantic Kernel, AutoGen, and Foundry into a unified Agent Framework resolves the framework selection debate and shifts focus to the harder architectural questions that actually determine production success. The article identifies the real challenges as: deciding when an agent is necessary versus a deterministic workflow, managing context scope deliberately, building robust failure recovery, and establishing identity and security boundaries. A cautionary case study illustrates how a four-step process built as an elaborate multi-agent system should have been a few well-tested functions.",
        "execSummary":  "The consolidation of Microsoft\u0027s agent frameworks is a clarifying moment for enterprise teams that stalled on framework selection ΓÇö that decision is now made, and the harder work begins. Karunanithi\u0027s core warning is directly applicable to customer support and operations deployments: the instinct to build multi-agent systems for complex workflows often produces systems that are harder to maintain and harder to trust than deterministic alternatives. The production resilience point is the one most organizations underinvest in ΓÇö agents that work in demos fail in production when edge cases, permission boundaries, or upstream API failures are not explicitly designed for. Enterprise leaders should apply a simple forcing question before approving any agentic project: could a well-tested deterministic function handle this, and if not, what specifically requires an agent?",
        "vendorLean":  "Microsoft",
