        "summary":  "Despite 65% of companies reporting AI-related incidents, most organizations lack effective enforcement controls even when policies exist. The article argues that successful AI governance requires focusing on high-risk use cases, implementing technical enforcement mechanisms, establishing continuous measurement, and assigning cross-functional ownership rather than producing comprehensive documentation. Policy without enforcement infrastructure is identified as the primary governance failure mode.",
        "execSummary":  "Most enterprise AI governance programs are documentation exercises, not operational controls ΓÇö and the gap between policy and enforcement is where incidents happen, which is an urgent problem as AI agents gain more autonomy in customer and business workflows. The article\u0027s prescription is practical: govern by risk tier, build technical guardrails, measure continuously, and assign accountable owners across functions rather than leaving it to IT alone. For customer-support leaders deploying AI agents, this means advocating for technical enforcement (output filters, escalation triggers, audit logs) alongside any policy rollout. The caveat: cross-functional governance requires executive sponsorship or it stalls at the committee stage.",
