        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "MIT Technology Review\u0027s recurring Hype Index column takes stock of AI developments that lack mainstream appeal, covering job displacement concerns, robotics advances from 1X, and critiques of consumer AI features including translation and smart glasses. The piece uses a light editorial lens to distinguish genuine AI progress from overhyped announcements, closing with a note on South Korean chip industry workers benefiting financially from the AI investment wave.",
        "execSummary":  "The Hype Index serves as a useful calibration tool for senior leaders trying to separate enterprise-relevant AI signal from consumer marketing noise. The job displacement thread is the most strategically relevant item for enterprise leaders: the volume and pace of workforce impact claims from AI deployments is accelerating, and organizations without a workforce transition narrative are increasingly exposed to employee and public relations risk. The broader point is that AI\u0027s most consequential near-term effects may be in unglamorous process automation rather than headline-grabbing product features. The key implication for enterprise strategy is to pay as much attention to the unsexy, high-ROI automation cases as to the AI capabilities generating media coverage.",
